   "source": [
    "## 5. Which THREE host stars have the MOST known planets?\n",
    "**Instruction:**\n",
    "Use only basic Python (lists, dictionaries, sorted).\n",
    "\n",
    "- Step1: convert the dictionary into a list of (star, planet_count) pairs\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- Step2: sort the list by planet count (highest first)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- Step3: select the top three stars\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- Step4: print the results\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "4c2fe100",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "[('TRAPPIST-1', 7), ('HD 110067', 6), ('K2-138', 6)]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#TODO: Determine the three host stars that have the most planets\n",
    "star_counts = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "for star in df[\"hostname\"]:\n",
    "    if star in star_counts:\n",
    "        star_counts[star] += 1\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        star_counts[star] = 1\n",
    "\n",
    "star_planets = list(star_counts.items())\n",
    "\n",
    "sorted_stars = sorted(star_planets, key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "top_three = sorted_stars[:3]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(top_three)"
   ]
  },
